# git-secrets allow-list — SAFE, minimal exceptions (verified with `git secrets --scan`).
#
# INVARIANT: an allow entry must never whitelist a line that could carry a real secret VALUE.
#   - We NEVER mirror a prohibited value pattern's matchable substring. (The old `sk-[A-Za-z0-9]`
#     entry matched the prefix of ANY sk-prefixed string and thereby blinded the prohibited
#     `sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}` pattern — removed.)
#   - We NEVER allow a `NAME`-equals-value assignment. (The old bare-name and name-with-equals
#     entries allow-listed a real assignment of a key name to a secret value — removed.)
# Only a documentation *mention* of a variable NAME — the name NOT immediately followed by an
# equals sign — is exempted here, so prose that names a key (e.g. ".env files contain XAI_API_KEY
# (...)", or "git secrets --add 'XAI_API_KEY'") passes the scan, while a real name-equals-secret
# assignment stays flagged. Verified: a synthetic assignment with a realistic secret is caught.
XAI_API_KEY([^=]|$)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY([^=]|$)
OPENAI_API_KEY([^=]|$)
